                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "The extent of racemization of L-amino acid residues to D-isomers in food proteins increases with pH, time, and temperature. The nutritional utilization of different D-amino acids vary widely, both in animals and humans. In addition, some D-amino acids may be deleterious. For example, although D-phenylalanine is nutritionally available as a source of L-phenylalanine, high concentrations of D-tyrosine inhibit the growth of mice. The antimetabolic effect of D-tyrosine can be minimized by increasing the L-phenylalanine content of the diet.",
