          {
            "TOCHeading": "Ecological Information",
            "Description": "This section provides information to evaluate the environmental impact of the chemical if it were released to the environment. The information may include data from toxicity tests performed on aquatic and/or terrestrial organisms, where available (e.g., acute or chronic aquatic toxicity data for fish, algae, and crustaceans; toxicity data on birds, bees, and plants).  This section also includes information on whether there is a potential for the chemical to persist or degrade in the environment either through biodegradation or other processes (such as oxidation or hydrolysis) as well as the concentration or amount of the chemical found in the environment (e.g., in plants, animals as well as in the atmosphere or water).",
            "Section": [
              {
                "TOCHeading": "Environmental Fate/Exposure Summary",
                "Description": "This section summarizes the environmental fate/exposure of this chemical, including the natural and artificial sources of the chemical as a pollutant, its environmental fate, biodegradation, abiotic degradation, bioconcentration, etc.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 53,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Tyrosine is a non-essential amino acid involved in microbial, plant and animal metabolism. It may be released to the environment via effluents at sites where it is produced and used in biochemical research or as a growth factor or dietary supplement in nutrition. Volatilization, and bioconcentration of tyrosine are not expected to be important environmental fate processes. Its adsorption to soil or sediment is difficult to predict. Soil grab sample data, using radiolabeled tyrosine, and several aqueous aerobic biological screening studies, which utilized settled waste water, sewage, or activated sludge for inocula, have demonstrated that tyrosine is readily biodegradable. The tyrosine molecule does not contain a chromophore that absorbs UV light in the environmentally significant range (>290 nm). Therefore, it should not be susceptible to direct photolysis in sunlit media. However, photo-oxidation with hydroxyl radicals may be important. A calculated half-life in full intensity sunlit water is 62 days. An estimated half-life for the vapor-phase reaction in air is 5 hrs. However if released to the atmosphere, tyrosine is expected to exist in the particulate phase. Dry deposition and precipitation may be important removal mechanisms. Occupational exposure, may occur through dermal contact or inhalation of dust at workplaces where tyrosine is produced or used. The most common non-occupational exposures are likely to result from the ingestion of tyrosine containing foods. (SRC)",
                          "Markup": [
