                          "String": "TERRESTRIAL FATE: With pK values of 2.20, 9.11 and 10.07 for the acid, base and phenol groups, respectively(1), tyrosine will exist in soil as series of ions in varying proportions that are pH dependent(2). Ions are not expected to volatilize and the soil adsorption of organic molecules with both negative and positive charges is difficult to predict(2,SRC). Soil grab sample data, using radiolabeled tyrosine(3), and several aqueous aerobic biological screening studies, which utilized settled waste water, sewage, or activated sludge for inocula, have demonstrated that tyrosine is readily biodegradable(4-6). The tyrosine molecule contains the amine functional group which may be susceptible to hydrolysis(2), but does not contain a chromophore that absorbs UV light in the environmentally significant range (>290 nm)(4). Therefore, it is not susceptible to direct photolysis in sunlit soil surfaces(SRC).",
