                    ],
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Tyrosine is a non-essential amino-acid found in both proteins and the free form in most living organisms(1). Soil grab sample data, using radiolabeled tyrosine and an acidic and basic Mollic Haploxeralf soils (Steinbeck loam and Greenfield sandy loam), demonstrated that tyrosine should biodegrade rapidly in the environment(2). Tests were incubated for 12 weeks at a temperature of 22 °C. In Greenfield sandy loam, mineralization rates (CO2 evolved) in 1, 8 and 12 weeks were 83, 90 and 92%, respectively. In Steinbeck loam, mineralization rates (CO2 evolved) in 1, 8 and 12 weeks were 80, 89 and 91%, respectively. Microbial uptake was also measured with overall utilization of the substrate. About 6.1% was incorporated into the microbial population of the Greenfield sandy loam. In Steinbeck loam. 2.9% was reported as microbial biomass. A number of aerobic biological screening studies, which utilized settled waste water, sewage, or activated sludge for inocula, have also demonstrated that tyrosine is readily biodegradable(2-5). A 5 day BOD was 64.7%(3). In 24 hrs, the BOD ranged from 22.6 to 30.7%(4-5).",
